  "summary": "Continuous, unbroken lines woven into intricate knots and spirals, forming complex patterns.",
  "description": "Celtic knotwork consists of endless interlacing strands without clear beginnings or ends, symbolizing eternity and interconnectedness. These designs emerged in early medieval manuscripts and stone carvings, evolving from earlier Pictish symbols and Roman influences.",
  "culturalContext": "Celtic knotwork — unbroken cord interlace — developed from Roman and Insular sources into the Insular Gospel-books' carpet pages (Kells, Lindisfarne), Pictish stones, and later Celtic Revival ornament (George Bain's 1951 construction manual revived it for jewelry, tattoos and logo design). The mathematical rule — one or few continuous strands with over-under alternation — makes it both ornament and meditation object; \"endless knot\" logic ties it to Christian eternity symbolism.",
